  "notes": "The sparse-and-wide Jeezy sound: what makes it is absence, so nearly every parameter here points down. The kick plays 1 and the \"and\" of 2 and nothing else, syncopation is the lowest on this bench, and the hats are straight 8ths with almost no rolls — `hihat.fill.prob` 0.15 against the genre's 0.55 is the single most identifying number in this file. Harmony is a drone: `chords.impliedByRiff` is set because the research says harmony is implied by a sustained pad rather than stated, and the i / i–VI pair is exactly what it names, not a reduction of something larger. The register gap is authored literally — the lead sits at 76–100 while the 808 sits at 16–30, and the middle is left empty for the ad-libs the research says are engineered into every second bar. ⚠ `bassline.rhythm` is `mirror_kick`, not a held note, and that is an engine limit rather than a reading of the record: the research describes a slow-attack, very-long-decay 808, and over a kick that plays twice a bar `mirror_kick` with `followRootsProb` 0.95 is the nearest honest thing the generator can produce. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium and says workflow interviews are limited — the inferred parameters are the melodic register bounds and `hihat.rolls.freqPerBar`, which the research characterises only as \"very few rolls\". ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
